WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.317
Welcome to the story deck for
Building Ticket-to-Machine,

2
00:00:03.317 --> 00:00:06.862
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.862 --> 00:00:11.031
Instead of eight chapters one at
a time, we'll follow one person's

4
00:00:11.031 --> 00:00:15.600
record all the way through Book 13's
loop on ServiceNow and SailPoint.

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.275
Her name is Erin, and she's fictional:
every value you'll see comes from the

6
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:25.188
kit's own test fixtures, and every command
was re-run before it went on a slide.

7
00:00:25.188 --> 00:00:29.963
Seven scenes: the drift run finds
her, her drift becomes a typed task,

8
00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:35.345
least-privilege clients touch it, we find
why it keeps coming back, the fix is

9
00:00:35.345 --> 00:00:37.342
actuated, a re-read closes it,

10
00:00:37.342 --> 00:00:41.075
and her closure becomes
evidence an I S S O can verify.

11
00:00:41.075 --> 00:00:44.950
Each scene ends with a command
to try and a one-question check.

12
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.562
One more thing before we start:
the code is illustrative.

13
00:00:48.562 --> 00:00:51.880
Its tests pass against
fixtures and mocked responses,

14
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.599
but it hasn't been run against a live
ServiceNow instance or SailPoint tenant.

15
00:00:56.599 --> 00:01:00.213
Where the vendors' documentation
doesn't settle a point,

16
00:01:00.213 --> 00:01:02.770
the workbook says so, and so will we.

17
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.645
Meet the cast.

18
00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:13.270
Erin's HR record says she works in
HR and reports to employee E1000.

19
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:20.458
Entra ID, in a Microsoft 365 GCC tenant,
says Finance, and a different manager.

20
00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:22.425
The directory audit shows why:

21
00:01:22.425 --> 00:01:27.146
on October twentieth a help desk
administrator typed Finance in by hand.

22
00:01:27.146 --> 00:01:29.107
SailPoint still says HR.

23
00:01:29.107 --> 00:01:31.094
And ServiceNow is the front door,

24
00:01:31.094 --> 00:01:34.407
where her drift becomes a
typed task with its evidence.

25
00:01:34.407 --> 00:01:37.947
The stakes are the two failures
the example guide warns about:

26
00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:40.652
closing her finding
because a ticket says done,

27
00:01:40.652 --> 00:01:44.746
and fixing her record by hand while
two systems still write department.

28
00:01:44.746 --> 00:01:48.482
Either way the drift comes back,
and nobody can prove what happened.

29
00:01:48.482 --> 00:01:55.103
Keep three numbers in your head: E1004
is Erin, E1000 is the manager HR names,

30
00:01:55.103 --> 00:02:01.071
and E1001, Alice, is the manager
Entra ID and SailPoint name instead.

31
00:02:01.071 --> 00:02:04.156
The estate is a FedRAMP
Moderate civilian one:

32
00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:07.892
ServiceNow's Government
Community Cloud, FedRAMP High;

33
00:02:07.892 --> 00:02:11.628
SailPoint's Identity Security
Cloud, FedRAMP Moderate;

34
00:02:11.628 --> 00:02:16.470
and Entra ID on the worldwide Graph
endpoint, which is what GCC uses.

35
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.208
Here's the route.

36
00:02:21.208 --> 00:02:24.955
Book 13's loop has five
steps: test, detect drift,

37
00:02:24.955 --> 00:02:29.096
raise a typed task, actuate,
and close with evidence.

38
00:02:29.096 --> 00:02:31.620
The example guide builds
it in seven gates,

39
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.145
and the workbook's
chapters build the code.

40
00:02:34.145 --> 00:02:38.132
Scene one is the drift
run, Gate 3 and Chapter 6.

41
00:02:38.132 --> 00:02:43.557
Scene two turns her drift into a
typed task, Gate 4, Chapters 1 and 4.

42
00:02:43.557 --> 00:02:48.270
Scene three is least privilege,
Gate 1, Chapters 2 and 3.

43
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:53.007
Scene four finds why her department
keeps drifting, Gate 2, Chapter 8.

44
00:02:53.007 --> 00:02:55.163
Scene five is actuation, Gate 5,

45
00:02:55.163 --> 00:02:58.957
which the kit's first pass
deliberately doesn't build.

46
00:02:58.957 --> 00:03:02.808
Scene six is the re-read,
Gate 6, Chapter 5.

47
00:03:02.808 --> 00:03:06.933
And scene seven is the
evidence, Gate 7, Chapter 7.

48
00:03:06.933 --> 00:03:08.883
Every scene has the same shape.

49
00:03:08.883 --> 00:03:11.984
First, the story beat: what
happens to Erin's record,

50
00:03:11.984 --> 00:03:16.220
with the code that handles it and
the federal requirement behind it.

51
00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.379
Then a slide called Try it,
with the exact Lab 1 command

52
00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:22.395
from the workbook and the
output it really printed.

53
00:03:22.395 --> 00:03:23.982
Then a one-question check.

54
00:03:23.982 --> 00:03:28.580
Pause the video if you want to answer
it; the answer opens the next slide.

55
00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:33.155
Scene one.

56
00:03:33.155 --> 00:03:38.017
On day one, the drift client joins
the HR extract, Microsoft Graph

57
00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:43.155
and SailPoint on the employee ID,
and measures Book 3's drift classes.

58
00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:44.980
Erin collects four findings:

59
00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.466
her Entra department and Entra manager
disagree with HR, her SailPoint

60
00:03:49.466 --> 00:03:54.180
manager disagrees too, and there's an
unauthorized write on her department.

61
00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.573
That last one appears only because
this run read the directory audit;

62
00:03:58.573 --> 00:04:01.193
without it, the class
isn't computed at all.

63
00:04:01.193 --> 00:04:05.403
Each finding's ID is a hash of
its class, system, employee,

64
00:04:05.403 --> 00:04:10.455
object and attribute, so the same
drift has the same ID every night.

65
00:04:10.455 --> 00:04:13.917
Why it matters: Gate 3
measures the starting drift.

66
00:04:13.917 --> 00:04:17.660
That's account management
and continuous monitoring,

67
00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.155
and the I S S O keeps every run's output.

68
00:04:20.155 --> 00:04:22.068
Notice what the run doesn't claim.

69
00:04:22.068 --> 00:04:26.265
Credential drift and schema drift
need other systems, the card system,

70
00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.726
Intune and the provisioning middleware,

71
00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:33.068
so every run lists them as not computed
instead of implying they're clean.

72
00:04:33.068 --> 00:04:37.020
And expect a burst of High findings
in a real estate's first month;

73
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.000
Book 3 calls that the measurement working.

74
00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.475
Try it.

75
00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:49.262
From the kit's folder, run the drift
client over the fixtures, with the audit.

76
00:04:49.262 --> 00:04:53.380
The summary counts four attribute
drifts, two missing identities,

77
00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:58.471
three orphan accounts, two stale accesses
and one unauthorized write, and it lists

78
00:04:58.471 --> 00:05:03.412
credential and schema drift as not computed
rather than implying they're clean.

79
00:05:03.412 --> 00:05:06.937
Then look for Erin's
employee ID in the CSV.

80
00:05:06.937 --> 00:05:11.641
There she is, four times:
department and manager in Entra ID,

81
00:05:11.641 --> 00:05:17.813
manager in SailPoint, each Medium at P3;
and the unauthorized write, High at P2.

82
00:05:17.813 --> 00:05:23.082
If you run this yourself, you need Python
3.10 or later with pytest and requests,

83
00:05:23.082 --> 00:05:25.799
and you work in the kit's examples folder.

84
00:05:25.799 --> 00:05:28.188
Nothing touches a tenant or the network.

85
00:05:28.188 --> 00:05:32.670
When you're done, delete the out
folder; the labs write everything there.

86
00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.232
Check one.

87
00:05:37.232 --> 00:05:43.358
Tomorrow, HR corrects Erin's department
to Finance, so HR and Entra ID agree.

88
00:05:43.358 --> 00:05:47.182
Which of her four day-one findings
still won't go away on its own?

89
00:05:47.182 --> 00:05:50.575
Is it the Entra department
drift, the Entra manager drift,

90
00:05:50.575 --> 00:05:53.895
the SailPoint manager drift,
or the unauthorized write?

91
00:05:53.895 --> 00:05:58.382
Think about what HR agreeing
afterwards does, and doesn't, prove.

92
00:05:58.382 --> 00:06:00.790
The answer opens the next scene.

93
00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.553
The answer to check one:
the unauthorized write.

94
00:06:07.553 --> 00:06:11.543
HR agreeing later doesn't make
a hand-typed change authorized,

95
00:06:11.543 --> 00:06:12.952
so a person decides it.

96
00:06:12.952 --> 00:06:13.816
Scene two.

97
00:06:13.816 --> 00:06:19.643
Each of Erin's findings becomes one typed
task in ServiceNow: a class, a severity,

98
00:06:19.643 --> 00:06:22.871
the system where it'll
be re-read, one subject,

99
00:06:22.871 --> 00:06:26.727
its origin, and the intended
state a re-read must find.

100
00:06:26.727 --> 00:06:29.916
Her department drift is P3, due in a day.

101
00:06:29.916 --> 00:06:35.070
The unauthorized write is P2, due in an
hour, and it's accepted with a warning,

102
00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:39.465
because its intended state is
empty until that person decides.

103
00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:42.592
From SailPoint's side, the
mover workflow raises her

104
00:06:42.592 --> 00:06:45.865
department change with
Finance as the intended state.

105
00:06:45.865 --> 00:06:51.140
Why it matters: Gate 4 makes every drift
an owned record answered inside a window.

106
00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:54.965
The tempo is Book 13's
design, not a federal clock.

107
00:06:54.965 --> 00:06:58.825
The important design choice is
that the rules live in one place,

108
00:06:58.825 --> 00:07:01.776
the scripted REST resource
inside ServiceNow,

109
00:07:01.776 --> 00:07:06.090
and the Python mirror is held to it
by a test that reads the JavaScript.

110
00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.714
So a malformed payload fails on a laptop,

111
00:07:08.714 --> 00:07:14.170
not as a four hundred from a live instance,
and the mirror can't quietly go stale.

112
00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:18.645
Try it.

113
00:07:18.645 --> 00:07:23.870
Feed day one's run to the contract check
at a fixed clock, noon on November first.

114
00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:28.883
Erin's department and manager drifts
are accepted at P3, due the next noon.

115
00:07:28.883 --> 00:07:32.878
Her unauthorized write is accepted
at P2, due at one o'clock,

116
00:07:32.878 --> 00:07:36.957
with the warning that its
intended state names nothing yet.

117
00:07:36.957 --> 00:07:38.919
All twelve payloads are accepted.

118
00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:41.430
Then resolve SailPoint's mover workflow

119
00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.383
against the fictional
event for Erin's identity.

120
00:07:44.383 --> 00:07:47.553
The intended state is
department Finance, P3,

121
00:07:47.553 --> 00:07:50.982
and the de-duplication
key ends in the attribute.

122
00:07:50.982 --> 00:07:54.333
This is the first thing to
check whenever a client changes:

123
00:07:54.333 --> 00:07:57.544
run its payloads through the
validator in its own tests.

124
00:07:57.544 --> 00:08:00.023
Nothing a client builds
should be rejected,

125
00:08:00.023 --> 00:08:02.707
and every warning should
be one you understand.

126
00:08:02.707 --> 00:08:06.779
Here, the only warnings are for
the classes a person decides first:

127
00:08:06.779 --> 00:08:10.150
missing identities and
the unauthorized write.

128
00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:14.725
Check two.

129
00:08:14.725 --> 00:08:18.787
Erin's department and manager
both drift in Entra ID.

130
00:08:18.787 --> 00:08:24.926
Before Chapter 4's fix, ServiceNow
de-duplicated on source, subject and class only.

131
00:08:24.926 --> 00:08:27.088
What went wrong with her two findings?

132
00:08:27.088 --> 00:08:32.920
Were they rejected, folded together,
given the wrong severity, or raised twice?

133
00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:40.362
The answer to check two: the
manager drift would have been folded

134
00:08:40.362 --> 00:08:43.845
into the department one, and its
intended state never re-read.

135
00:08:43.845 --> 00:08:46.707
Scene three: who may touch Erin's record.

136
00:08:46.707 --> 00:08:50.327
The drift client read her through
SailPoint with two read scopes,

137
00:08:50.327 --> 00:08:54.483
and through Graph with the user read
permission plus the audit permission.

138
00:08:54.483 --> 00:08:58.408
That audit read is the only reason
the help desk write was seen.

139
00:08:58.408 --> 00:09:01.774
In ServiceNow, a finding
writer posted her tasks,

140
00:09:01.774 --> 00:09:06.570
only the verifier may close them,
and an evidence reader may only read.

141
00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:12.033
Every call is a row in one file, and a
test fails on any call it doesn't declare.

142
00:09:12.033 --> 00:09:17.176
Why it matters: Gate 1 records the
integration identities, each least-privileged,

143
00:09:17.176 --> 00:09:22.396
with certificates rather than secrets,
and the closer separated from the raisers.

144
00:09:22.396 --> 00:09:25.370
Two details from Chapter
3 are worth carrying.

145
00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:29.231
A SailPoint client-credentials
token has no user context,

146
00:09:29.231 --> 00:09:34.137
and SailPoint warns that most admin
APIs often return forbidden with one,

147
00:09:34.137 --> 00:09:37.595
so Lab 2 records what actually
works in your sandbox.

148
00:09:37.595 --> 00:09:41.562
And the scope that creates
workflows lives on its own client,

149
00:09:41.562 --> 00:09:43.420
used once, then removed.

150
00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:47.895
Try it.

151
00:09:47.895 --> 00:09:50.468
Suppose the SailPoint
reader that read Erin

152
00:09:50.468 --> 00:09:52.833
was also given the accounts manage scope.

153
00:09:52.833 --> 00:09:56.506
Compare it with what the drift
task needs: nothing missing,

154
00:09:56.506 --> 00:10:01.021
the manage scope extra, least privilege
doesn't hold, exit with a failure.

155
00:10:01.021 --> 00:10:03.695
A stolen token could have
changed her accounts.

156
00:10:03.695 --> 00:10:08.819
Now the ServiceNow verifier, given
the broad I T I L role as well:

157
00:10:08.819 --> 00:10:11.732
same result, one extra role, failure.

158
00:10:11.732 --> 00:10:16.015
The tool calls no network; the
operator types or exports the grant,

159
00:10:16.015 --> 00:10:19.108
and the output goes beside
the access approval.

160
00:10:19.108 --> 00:10:21.420
The same comparison works for Graph.

161
00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:26.388
An app registration holding only the user
read permission fails a drift run that

162
00:10:26.388 --> 00:10:31.207
reads the audit, and passes one that doesn't
when you ask for required calls only.

163
00:10:31.207 --> 00:10:33.899
In Erin's case, the
audit read is required,

164
00:10:33.899 --> 00:10:38.080
because it's the only way her
help desk write was ever seen.

165
00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:42.742
Check three.

166
00:10:42.742 --> 00:10:46.618
Chapter 7's evidence export
reads Erin's closed record.

167
00:10:46.618 --> 00:10:50.006
It would work perfectly well
if it ran as the verifier.

168
00:10:50.006 --> 00:10:53.000
So why does the comparison
refuse that grant?

169
00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:01.013
The answer to check three: running as
the verifier, the export could also

170
00:11:01.013 --> 00:11:05.275
close the findings it's about to hash,
which breaks separation of duties.

171
00:11:05.275 --> 00:11:08.300
Scene four asks why Erin's
department drifts at all.

172
00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:13.192
Book 3 makes Entra inbound provisioning
the one writer into the directory,

173
00:11:13.192 --> 00:11:15.875
but SailPoint can provision Entra ID too.

174
00:11:15.875 --> 00:11:19.199
If both update department,
each overwrites the other,

175
00:11:19.199 --> 00:11:22.900
and fixing her record by hand
just schedules the next drift.

176
00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.262
The writer check compares the two
provisioning configurations, offline.

177
00:11:27.262 --> 00:11:30.621
The kit's exports are fictional
files, not Erin's tenant,

178
00:11:30.621 --> 00:11:35.025
but they show exactly the overlap that
would keep her drift coming back.

179
00:11:35.025 --> 00:11:38.640
Why it matters: Gate 2 decides
one writer per attribute,

180
00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:41.613
so every account change
traces to its source.

181
00:11:41.613 --> 00:11:46.167
This is the one item from the kit's planned
second pass that can be built today,

182
00:11:46.167 --> 00:11:50.576
because both configurations export
as JSON through documented reads.

183
00:11:50.576 --> 00:11:55.767
The other planned items, one approver per
authority class, change records on demand,

184
00:11:55.767 --> 00:11:59.003
the SailPoint half of the
evidence and email intake,

185
00:11:59.003 --> 00:12:02.230
all need a live instance
or product internals.

186
00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:06.705
Try it.

187
00:12:06.705 --> 00:12:09.705
Run the writer check on
the two fictional exports.

188
00:12:09.705 --> 00:12:14.768
Entra inbound provisioning writes eight
attributes, SailPoint writes seven,

189
00:12:14.768 --> 00:12:17.494
and department is a
Finding: both update it,

190
00:12:17.494 --> 00:12:21.155
so each overwrites the other
and the drift never settles.

191
00:12:21.155 --> 00:12:23.430
That's Erin's problem in one line.

192
00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:28.042
Display name is a warning, and two
attributes are only informational.

193
00:12:28.042 --> 00:12:31.153
Now map SailPoint's title
field to Entra's job

194
00:12:31.153 --> 00:12:34.431
title and run it again:
a second Finding appears.

195
00:12:34.431 --> 00:12:37.730
Without the alias, that
second writer was invisible.

196
00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:40.018
And notice the guard
on the directory name.

197
00:12:40.018 --> 00:12:43.487
If you tell the check the wrong
name for the Entra directory,

198
00:12:43.487 --> 00:12:47.452
it refuses and exits with an error,
rather than reporting no overlap,

199
00:12:47.452 --> 00:12:51.570
because a wrong name would make
every attribute look unwritten.

200
00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:56.232
Check four.

201
00:12:56.232 --> 00:13:00.378
Entra inbound provisioning updates
display name on every change,

202
00:13:00.378 --> 00:13:03.770
and SailPoint sets it only
when it creates the account.

203
00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:09.490
What does the writer check report: a
Finding, a warning, info, or nothing at all?

204
00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:15.239
The answer to check four: a warning,

205
00:13:15.239 --> 00:13:18.165
because there are two
sources for the first value.

206
00:13:18.165 --> 00:13:22.103
Scene five is the fix itself, and
here the kit stops on purpose.

207
00:13:22.103 --> 00:13:23.777
The guide says how it should run.

208
00:13:23.777 --> 00:13:27.859
Erin's wrong fact is corrected
where HR owns it, in Core HCM,

209
00:13:27.859 --> 00:13:33.590
and SailPoint re-aggregates; SailPoint
can't write HR-mastered attributes back.

210
00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:36.833
Her unauthorized write is
restored by SailPoint,

211
00:13:36.833 --> 00:13:39.127
and the writer's access is reviewed.

212
00:13:39.127 --> 00:13:42.221
The ticket authorizes
a machine: a connector,

213
00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:45.816
under a service identity,
and a person only approves.

214
00:13:45.816 --> 00:13:49.025
But the kit's first pass
doesn't actuate at all:

215
00:13:49.025 --> 00:13:53.102
no code writes to Entra ID,
Active Directory or SailPoint.

216
00:13:53.102 --> 00:13:56.516
What it does have is the closure
signal, which we'll try next.

217
00:13:56.516 --> 00:14:00.765
Why it matters: Gate 5 keeps
changes off personal admin accounts.

218
00:14:00.765 --> 00:14:03.690
The guide also keeps people
off standing privilege.

219
00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:08.069
Where a person must act, they activate
an eligible role just in time,

220
00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:11.015
with the ticket number
as the justification.

221
00:14:11.015 --> 00:14:16.255
And when no machine can reach the surface,
the declared-exception lane takes over:

222
00:14:16.255 --> 00:14:19.900
a named approver, a recorded
session and an end date.

223
00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:24.375
Try it.

224
00:14:24.375 --> 00:14:27.122
The kit's provisioning fixture is Alice's,

225
00:14:27.122 --> 00:14:30.112
not Erin's, but the shape
is the same for anyone.

226
00:14:30.112 --> 00:14:33.600
Resolve SailPoint's provisioning-completed
workflow against it.

227
00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:38.975
ServiceNow gets a closure signal,
accepted, with four input fields not sent.

228
00:14:38.975 --> 00:14:41.130
And here's what a closure signal does:

229
00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:44.363
it moves matching findings
to awaiting verification.

230
00:14:44.363 --> 00:14:45.812
It never closes one.

231
00:14:45.812 --> 00:14:48.244
That's the example guide's first gap,

232
00:14:48.244 --> 00:14:51.849
written into code: a success
response is not closure.

233
00:14:51.849 --> 00:14:56.363
The workflow is also why provisioning
status can't be trusted on its own.

234
00:14:56.363 --> 00:14:59.830
SailPoint's own best-practice
page says its Service Desk

235
00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:03.813
integration treats the mapped
ServiceNow status as completion.

236
00:15:03.813 --> 00:15:09.690
The kit turns that around: a status or a
success only starts the wait for evidence.

237
00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:14.378
Check five.

238
00:15:14.378 --> 00:15:17.621
SailPoint reports success
for the fix to Erin's record,

239
00:15:17.621 --> 00:15:20.216
and the closure signal reaches ServiceNow.

240
00:15:20.216 --> 00:15:21.802
What happens to her finding?

241
00:15:21.802 --> 00:15:26.670
Does it close, wait for a
re-read, get ignored, or reopen?

242
00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:31.947
The answer to check five:

243
00:15:31.947 --> 00:15:36.145
her finding moves to awaiting
verification, and waits for a re-read.

244
00:15:36.145 --> 00:15:37.232
Scene six.

245
00:15:37.232 --> 00:15:39.401
On day two, Entra ID matches HR,

246
00:15:39.401 --> 00:15:44.732
so the reconciliation calls that
finding resolved, which is only a lead.

247
00:15:44.732 --> 00:15:50.033
SailPoint still says HR until its next
aggregation, so a new finding opens there.

248
00:15:50.033 --> 00:15:55.224
When it's re-read, waiting record I D
F one thousand four, Erin's SailPoint

249
00:15:55.224 --> 00:16:00.595
identity with intended department
Finance, reads Finance, and verifies.

250
00:16:00.595 --> 00:16:02.495
The client guards both ways.

251
00:16:02.495 --> 00:16:06.433
An intended state that names
nothing is skipped, never verified.

252
00:16:06.433 --> 00:16:10.224
And a read older than the wait is
pending through a grace period,

253
00:16:10.224 --> 00:16:11.682
then reopens the record.

254
00:16:11.682 --> 00:16:15.820
Why it matters: Gate 6,
Book 13's fourth clause.

255
00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:19.403
The record closes only on a
re-read, with its evidence,

256
00:16:19.403 --> 00:16:22.920
and no person can close
it by setting its state.

257
00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:27.395
Try it.

258
00:16:27.395 --> 00:16:31.542
Compare day one with day two:
one new, eleven persisting,

259
00:16:31.542 --> 00:16:34.583
one resolved, and both changes are Erin's.

260
00:16:34.583 --> 00:16:38.170
Her Entra finding resolved;
a SailPoint finding opened.

261
00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:41.022
Then run the re-read
client offline over six

262
00:16:41.022 --> 00:16:43.795
waiting records at noon on November first.

263
00:16:43.795 --> 00:16:47.183
Erin's record verifies, and
would be patched to closed.

264
00:16:47.183 --> 00:16:50.476
One record is pending: a
terminated user's account,

265
00:16:50.476 --> 00:16:53.207
read before the finding started waiting.

266
00:16:53.207 --> 00:16:55.857
One is skipped for an
empty intended state.

267
00:16:55.857 --> 00:16:57.826
Run the same records a day later,

268
00:16:57.826 --> 00:17:01.383
and that pending one becomes
a mismatch, and would reopen.

269
00:17:01.383 --> 00:17:03.195
Everything here is a dry run.

270
00:17:03.195 --> 00:17:07.257
The offline mode refuses the
apply flag, and a subject with no

271
00:17:07.257 --> 00:17:11.320
saved read is an error, because
a missing read is never a match.

272
00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:13.116
On a developer instance,

273
00:17:13.116 --> 00:17:17.970
Chapter 5's Lab 2 runs the same
client for real, as the verifier.

274
00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:22.745
Check six.

275
00:17:22.745 --> 00:17:28.020
On day two, the comparison reports Erin's
Entra department finding as resolved.

276
00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.275
Can its ServiceNow record close now,

277
00:17:30.275 --> 00:17:33.733
because it's resolved, or
because a person sets it closed?

278
00:17:33.733 --> 00:17:36.300
Or does something else
have to happen first?

279
00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:42.038
The answer to check six: no.

280
00:17:42.038 --> 00:17:46.426
Resolved is a lead; only a matching
re-read closes the record.

281
00:17:46.426 --> 00:17:47.475
Scene seven.

282
00:17:47.475 --> 00:17:52.325
When the re-read verified Erin's record,
it wrote the result with an evidence hash.

283
00:17:52.325 --> 00:17:58.086
On a real instance, that closed record is
read back out and written as one line of

284
00:17:58.086 --> 00:18:01.366
an evidence package, in
Book 13's shape: origin,

285
00:18:01.366 --> 00:18:05.000
owner and clock, the re-read
result, and the closure.

286
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:09.325
Approval, the executing identity
and what ran are written as null,

287
00:18:09.325 --> 00:18:13.250
each with its reason, because
the first pass doesn't actuate.

288
00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:15.375
The record says what it doesn't hold.

289
00:18:15.375 --> 00:18:18.537
Each line carries the hash
of the line before it,

290
00:18:18.537 --> 00:18:21.213
and a manifest holds a hash of every file.

291
00:18:21.213 --> 00:18:23.237
Why it matters: Gate 7.

292
00:18:23.237 --> 00:18:28.010
The I S S O gets records nobody could
edit or remove without it showing.

293
00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:32.485
Try it.

294
00:18:32.485 --> 00:18:35.847
The lab's package uses five
other fictional records,

295
00:18:35.847 --> 00:18:38.897
but Erin's would be one
more line in the same file.

296
00:18:38.897 --> 00:18:43.175
Export October: four closed on a
re-read, one declared exception,

297
00:18:43.175 --> 00:18:46.548
two of three inside their
windows, and a chain head.

298
00:18:46.548 --> 00:18:50.228
It also names three problem
records and exits with a failure,

299
00:18:50.228 --> 00:18:53.098
rather than producing a
clean-looking package.

300
00:18:53.098 --> 00:18:57.509
Now verify it as the I S S
O would: the package holds.

301
00:18:57.509 --> 00:19:00.273
Then delete one record and verify again.

302
00:19:00.273 --> 00:19:05.309
The file's hash no longer matches the
manifest, and the next line's link is broken.

303
00:19:05.309 --> 00:19:06.935
The package doesn't hold.

304
00:19:06.935 --> 00:19:11.068
Next month's export continues the
chain from this month's manifest,

305
00:19:11.068 --> 00:19:13.172
so a gap between periods shows too.

306
00:19:13.172 --> 00:19:17.397
One limit to remember: the package
is tamper-evident, not signed.

307
00:19:17.397 --> 00:19:21.870
Someone who can rewrite every file and
the manifest can rewrite the chain,

308
00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:25.390
so keep each manifest where
the exporter can't write.

309
00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:30.977
Check seven, the last one.

310
00:19:30.977 --> 00:19:34.765
Someone deletes one closed record
from October's records file.

311
00:19:34.765 --> 00:19:39.216
What does the verifier report:
nothing, only a file hash mismatch,

312
00:19:39.216 --> 00:19:43.190
the file hash and a broken
chain, or a moved chain head?

313
00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:51.133
The answer to check seven: the file's
hash no longer matches the manifest,

314
00:19:51.133 --> 00:19:53.265
and the chain breaks at the next line.

315
00:19:53.265 --> 00:19:57.437
That's Erin's record, end to end:
measured, typed and clocked,

316
00:19:57.437 --> 00:20:02.141
touched only by least-privileged
clients, traced to two writers,

317
00:20:02.141 --> 00:20:06.490
fixed at the source, closed on a
re-read, and filed as evidence.

318
00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:07.627
Now it's your turn.

319
00:20:07.627 --> 00:20:10.645
Every chapter has a Lab 2
in your own test instance:

320
00:20:10.645 --> 00:20:14.546
a ServiceNow Personal Developer
Instance, a SailPoint sandbox,

321
00:20:14.546 --> 00:20:19.403
and for some chapters a test Entra tenant
with synthetic data, never production.

322
00:20:19.403 --> 00:20:22.412
There's no hosted sandbox;
you bring your own,

323
00:20:22.412 --> 00:20:25.003
and every lab ends with a back-out step.

324
00:20:25.003 --> 00:20:29.400
Then go deeper: the workbook's eight
chapters, a narrated tour of each,

325
00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:34.427
the seven-module learning path, and the
example guide with its gate-level deck.

326
00:20:34.427 --> 00:20:36.040
Thanks for following Erin.
